From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Er Gibbio <gibbio@pm.me>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 13:05:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/21] audio: add a CoreAudio backend for macOS

GStreamer's osxaudiosink buffers 200 ms by default, so sound came ~220 ms
after it arrived and, with the guest playing sound for a while, the video
followed it there. It also stays on the output device it opened: plugging
in headphones during a session left the sound on the speakers.

Play through the system's default output unit, which follows the default
device by itself, from a ring buffer that starts playing once its target is
buffered and again after it runs dry. The target starts at
SPICE_COREAUDIO_BUFFER_MS (default 40). Packets normally come every ~17 ms,
but now and then the server's audio pauses, up to ~90 ms, and then comes at
once: with a fixed 40 ms the output ran dry 11 times in 18 min. So the
target grows, up to 200 ms, to 10 ms above any pause that comes near it,
and by 20 ms after an underrun all the same, and shrinks by 10 ms after
every 5 calm minutes. Audio that stays buffered beyond the target for a
whole second is skipped, so clock drift and bursts do not add up to delay. The delay given
to the session is what is buffered plus the device's own latency, from the
moment it is known. Record through an audio queue on the default input
device, reopened when that changes.

The microphone is opened only while the "record-enabled" property is set:
the guest can start recording at any time. Read-only properties report
whether it is recording, the buffered audio, underruns, skipped audio and
the output latency.

Calls that wait for the device (creating, starting, stopping units and
queues) run on a serial dispatch queue, off the main context.
SPICE_AUDIO_BACKEND=gstreamer keeps the GStreamer backend, without a record
channel: it has no such switch and would open the microphone whenever the
guest records.
---
